<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cesare d'Este, Duke of Modena</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1562">1562</a>–<a href="/years/1628">1628</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Cesare d'Este</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cesare d'Este was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and Reggio from 1597. As cousin and heir of Duke Alfonso II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, he expected to inherit Ferrara too, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-viii">Pope Clement VIII</a> refused to recognise him, arguing that his line was illegitimate, and in 1598 Ferrara was annexed to the Papal States. He moved the Este court to Modena, which remained its capital until 1859.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cesare-d-este-duke-of-modena.jpg" alt="Cesare d'Este, Duke of Modena" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frans Pourbus the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cesare_d%E2%80%99Este.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
